-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction Policy
+
+The cache uses a 2Q (Two Queue) eviction policy to protect repeatedly-accessed comparison sheets during long sequential screenings. Entries flow through two queues:
+
+- **A1 (first-use queue)**: Newly-rendered sheets enter here. Maximum size is 3 GiB (25% of total cache).
+- **Am (reused queue)**: Sheets accessed again while in A1 move here. Maximum size is 9 GiB (75% of total cache).
+
+When admitting a new sheet would exceed capacity, evict from A1 tail first. If A1 is empty or the new sheet is larger than available A1 space, evict from Am tail. A sheet promoted from A1 to Am uses Am's capacity; if Am is full, evict from Am tail before promotion.
+
+On cache access, if the sheet is in A1, move it to Am head and record the promotion. If the sheet is in Am, move it to Am head. Misses render and admit to A1 head.
+
+This structure allows a long sequential scan (thousands of one-use sheets) to churn through A1 without displacing the smaller working set editors compare repeatedly in Am. The 25/75 split reserves enough A1 space to absorb sequential bursts while dedicating most capacity to proven reuse.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (two-queue LRU variant) | LRU, Frequency-based with aging | 2Q protects repeatedly-accessed comparison sheets from eviction during long sequential screenings. LRU has lower bookkeeping cost and adapts quickly when editors switch projects, but a sequential scan of thousands of clips evicts the small working set editors compare side-by-side. Frequency-based eviction with aging retains popular clips longer, but requires counter maintenance and aging logic, and can keep yesterday's favorites warm when today's project has shifted. 2Q accepts the overhead of two queues and a tunable size split to resist scan interference, which matches the observed workload pattern of alternating long screenings with repeated comparisons. |
+| A1/Am size split | 25% A1, 75% Am | 50/50, 10/90 | 25% A1 gives enough headroom to absorb a sequential screening burst (hundreds of one-use sheets) without immediately evicting from Am, while 75% Am dedicates most capacity to the working set that sees repeated access. A 50/50 split would allow scans to evict half the reused set; 10/90 would cause A1 to overflow into Am evictions too quickly during normal screenings. |
